        "thinking": "The user is asking about the production deployed version on Cloudflare. In production:\n\n1. There's no local Node.js server\n2. The `SIDECAR_SERVICE` DO is backed by a CF Container running the sidecar Docker image (`ghcr.io/paperclipai/paperclip-sidecar:latest`)\n3. Plugin install and examples need to work via that container\n\nSo the question is: what does the sidecar container do in production? It runs the Node.js server (the companion server), which includes:\n- Plugin install (`POST /api/plugins/install`)\n- Plugin examples (`GET /api/plugins/examples`)\n\n\n- Heartbeat execution and other Node.js operations\n\nThe container exposes port 8080, which the CF Worker accesses via `this.ctx.container.getTcpPort(8080)`. For plugin installation to work, the container needs npm available, a writable filesystem for installations, and access to the `~/.paperclip/plugins` directory. The plugin examples endpoint checks for bundled examples in the filesystem.\n\nIn production, plugin examples would return empty since the monorepo isn't present in the container. Plugin installation via npm could work if the container has npm and write access, but there's a critical issue: CF Containers have ephemeral storage, so any installed plugins would disappear on restart—only the database record would persist, not the actual npm package files.\n\nA better production approach would be storing plugin bundles in R2 and loading them at startup, but that requires a different plugin loading mechanism. For now, the deployed version would route plugin install requests through the sidecar container's Node.js server, though this has the ephemeral storage limitation.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
